SLAG-AR: Simple Lightweight Archive & Generated-manifest Architecture for Recipes
================================================================================

Package Format (.fpm)
---------------------

A .fpm package is a standard tar archive containing:

  fpm.meta       - Package metadata (KEY=VALUE format)
  fpm.manifest   - File manifest with checksums
  <file tree>    - Actual package files

All file paths in the archive are relative to the installation root (typically /).


fpm.meta Format
---------------

  NAME=<package name>
  VERSION=<version string>
  ARCH=<architecture (x86_64, aarch64, etc.)>
  REPO=<repository (core, extra, etc.)>
  SIZE=<human-readable size>
  DESC=<description>
  SHA256=<SHA256 hash of the .fpm archive itself>
  DEPENDS=<comma-separated dependency list>


fpm.manifest Format
-------------------

Each line: <mode> <size> <sha256> <path>

Example:
  100755 12345 abcd1234... /usr/bin/myapp
  100644  1234 ef567890... /usr/share/doc/myapp/README


Local Database Structure
------------------------

Installed package info: /var/lib/fpm/local/<package>/
  meta       - KEY=VALUE metadata (same fields as fpm.meta minus SHA256)
  manifest   - List of installed files (one path per line)

Repository cache: /var/cache/fpm/repo/<repo>.db
Package cache:    /var/cache/fpm/packages/


Repository Database Format (.db)
--------------------------------

Pipe-delimited, one package per line:

  <name> | <version> | <arch> | <repo> | <size> | <description> | <sha256> | <depends>


Design Principles
-----------------

1. Zero external C++ dependencies (uses system tar/sha256sum/curl)
2. Lightweight: minimal metadata, fast parsing
3. Human-readable: all metadata files are plain text
4. Verifiable: SHA256 checksums for archive and file integrity
5. Compatible: tar format allows standard tools to inspect packages
